    "id": "case-vte",
    "title": "Postoperative hip arthroplasty — venous thromboembolism",
    "clinicalCategory": "Physiological Adaptation",
    "origin": "original",
    "scenario": "The nurse is caring for a 68-year-old client on an orthopaedic unit on postoperative day 2 after an elective right total hip arthroplasty.",
    "totalParts": 6,
    "records": [
      {
        "id": "hp",
        "label": "History and Physical",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "heading": "History",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Right hip osteoarthritis with progressive pain. Hypertension treated with amlodipine. Body mass index 34 kg/m². Former smoker (quit 6 years ago). No previous venous thromboembolism.",
              "Allergies: no known drug allergies."
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "heading": "Surgery",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Day 0: right total hip arthroplasty under spinal anaesthesia. Estimated blood loss 350 mL. No intraoperative complications."
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "notes",
        "label": "Nurses' Notes",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "entries",
            "entries": [
              {
                "time": "Day 2, 08:00",
                "text": "Client walked 10 metres with physical therapy using a walker. Reports the right calf feels \"tight and aching\" when walking. Right calf circumference 3 cm larger than left; right calf warm and tender to touch. Pedal pulses 2+ bilaterally; capillary refill less than 3 seconds in both feet. Hip incision edges approximated, no drainage. Hip pain 4/10 one hour after oxycodone. Lungs clear bilaterally. Client states he declined the enoxaparin injection last evening because \"the shots leave bruises.\""
              },
              {
                "time": "Day 2, 10:30",
                "fromPart": 3,
                "text": "Client called out, stating \"I suddenly can't catch my breath.\" Reports sharp right-sided chest pain, 8/10, worse on inspiration. Restless and anxious, skin diaphoretic. Respirations rapid and shallow. Lungs clear bilaterally. Oxygen saturation 88% on room air."
              },
              {
                "time": "Day 2, 11:30",
                "fromPart": 6,
                "text": "Oxygen at 4 L/min by nasal cannula. Heparin infusion running per protocol. Client states \"breathing is much easier.\" Pain on inspiration 2/10. Calmer, skin dry. Right calf circumference unchanged from 08:00 measurement; calf remains warm and tender."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
